## Thread.Abort → .NET 8 Migration

### Why This Breaks

`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 6+. `ThreadAbortException` is never thrown, so your catch block and `ResetAbort()` are dead code. The `Stop()` method will crash at runtime.

### Pattern Classification

| Usage | Pattern | Replacement |
|-------|---------|-------------|
| `_workerThread.Abort()` | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Control flow cleanup | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Continue after abort | Check `token.IsCancellationRequested` |
| `Thread.Sleep(2000)` in Stop | Blocking wait | `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne` + `Thread.Join(timeout)` |

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        var token = _cts.Token;
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock waits
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Cancellation requested — clean up partial work
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed. Loop condition will exit naturally.
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5)); // wait for clean exit
        // No Thread.Abort fallback — cooperative cancellation is the only option
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
        _cts = null;
    }
}
```

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | What to do |
|---------|------------|
| **`GetNextItem()` blocks forever** | It must accept `CancellationToken` and use cancellable waits (e.g., `BlockingCollection.Take(token)`, `SemaphoreSlim.Wait(token)`). If it ignores the token, `Stop()` hangs. |
| **`Thread.Sleep` inside work** | Replace with `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(ms)` — this wakes immediately on cancellation. |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks timers/callbacks. Implement `IDisposable` as shown. |
| **Catching `OperationCanceledException` too broadly** | Use `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` filter to avoid swallowing unrelated exceptions. |
| **Assuming cancellation is instant** | It only fires at the next checkpoint. Add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` between expensive steps inside `ProcessItem`. |
| **No fallback for truly stuck threads** | If `Join(timeout)` expires, the thread is stuck in uncooperative code. Move that work to a child `Process` and use `Process.Kill()` as a last resort. |

### If `GetNextItem()` Currently Blocks on a `ManualResetEvent`

```csharp
// BEFORE (.NET Framework)
_event.WaitOne(); // blocks until signaled — Thread.Abort was the escape hatch

// AFTER (.NET 8)
WaitHandle.WaitAny(new[] { _event, token.WaitHandle }); // wakes on either signal
token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
```

The fundamental shift: **.NET Framework let you yank the rug out** (`Abort`). **.NET 8 requires the thread to check and cooperate.** Every blocking call in the chain must accept and respect the `CancellationToken`.
